          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em canonical morphism
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            which is equivariant under an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            mediated by two
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            in a particular context. Conversely, canonical morphisms can be combined into a notion of transformation more general than a natural transformation, although this also may be seen as a natural transformation in a particular context.
          %p
            This usage of the word ‘canonical’ is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Dolan"} Jim Dolan
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            are related to avoiding the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            Arguably, ‘natural’ would be a better term for this intuition, but canonical morphisms are more general than the natural transformations that appear in the same contexts, so that word is taken. Another possible term is ‘core-natural’ or ‘groupoid-natural’, since (as will be seen below) canonical morphisms may be interpreted as natural transformations between functors restricted to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            (underlying
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definable+set"} definable set
            (in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parametric+polymorphism"} parametric polymorphism
            (in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
